00:00:07,111 --> 00:00:09,308
on its own official books.

5
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:11,364
But at the market price of gold

6
00:00:11,364 --> 00:00:13,404
at the end of September twenty

7
00:00:13,404 --> 00:00:14,288
twenty-five,

8
00:00:14,288 --> 00:00:16,079
the very same gold was worth,

9
00:00:16,079 --> 00:00:17,643
by our calculation,

10
00:00:17,643 --> 00:00:19,830
about one trillion dollars.

11
00:00:20,050 --> 00:00:23,700
Nearly a hundred times more.

12
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:24,256
Same bars,

13
00:00:24,256 --> 00:00:25,239
same vaults.

14
00:00:25,483 --> 00:00:28,536
So why does the world's biggest economy

15
00:00:28,536 --> 00:00:30,479
still value its gold like it is

16
00:00:30,479 --> 00:00:33,022
nineteen seventy-three?

17
00:00:33,283 --> 00:00:35,396
By the end of this video,

18
00:00:35,396 --> 00:00:37,535
you will know where that gold really

19
00:00:37,535 --> 00:00:38,043
sits,

20
00:00:38,043 --> 00:00:40,183
why it still carries a price from

21
00:00:40,183 --> 00:00:41,493
the seventies,

22
00:00:41,493 --> 00:00:43,873
who checks that it is really there,

23
00:00:43,873 --> 00:00:46,548
and what could happen if someone rewrote

24
00:00:46,548 --> 00:00:47,683
the price.

25
00:00:53,317 --> 00:00:56,017
Start with the pile itself.

26
00:00:56,057 --> 00:00:57,971
According to the Treasury,

27
00:00:57,971 --> 00:01:00,337
the government owns about two hundred and

28
00:01:00,337 --> 00:01:02,657
sixty-one million ounces of gold.

29
00:01:02,957 --> 00:01:04,476
By our calculation,

30
00:01:04,476 --> 00:01:06,677
that is about eight thousand one hundred

31
00:01:06,677 --> 00:01:07,717
tonnes.

32
00:01:07,817 --> 00:01:10,048
And the number has barely moved in

33
00:01:10,048 --> 00:01:11,107
decades.

34
00:01:11,107 --> 00:01:13,600
A congressional audit office counted

35
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:16,320
almost exactly the same amount in twenty

36
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:17,146
ten.

37
00:01:17,383 --> 00:01:19,366
Most of it is not where you

38
00:01:19,366 --> 00:01:20,533
might think.

39
00:01:20,543 --> 00:01:21,191
Fort Knox,

40
00:01:21,191 --> 00:01:22,286
in Kentucky,

41
00:01:22,286 --> 00:01:24,673
holds about fifty-six percent.

42
00:01:24,673 --> 00:01:25,355
West Point,

43
00:01:25,355 --> 00:01:26,272
in New York,

44
00:01:26,272 --> 00:01:28,283
about twenty-one percent.

45
00:01:28,293 --> 00:01:29,505
The Mint in Denver,

46
00:01:29,505 --> 00:01:31,543
about seventeen percent.

47
00:01:31,563 --> 00:01:33,607
And about five percent sits in a

48
00:01:33,607 --> 00:01:36,106
vault under the Federal Reserve Bank of

49
00:01:36,106 --> 00:01:37,160
New York.

50
00:01:37,417 --> 00:01:39,531
Almost all of it sits in what

51
00:01:39,531 --> 00:01:41,355
the Mint calls deep storage,

52
00:01:41,355 --> 00:01:43,734
sealed vaults that are not touched for

53
00:01:43,734 --> 00:01:45,127
daily work.

54
00:01:45,387 --> 00:01:48,100
The Treasury's inspector general notes

55
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:49,909
that the Mint did not use any

56
00:01:49,909 --> 00:01:51,718
of that deep storage gold to make

57
00:01:51,718 --> 00:01:53,979
coins in twenty twenty-four or twenty

58
00:01:53,979 --> 00:01:55,257
twenty-five.

59
00:01:55,467 --> 00:01:57,707
The gold is not inventory.

60
00:01:57,827 --> 00:01:59,995
It is a reserve that simply sits

61
00:01:59,995 --> 00:02:00,453
there,

62
00:02:00,453 --> 00:02:02,016
year after year.

63
00:02:02,250 --> 00:02:04,276
The New York slice is small,

64
00:02:04,276 --> 00:02:06,050
but not trivial.

65
00:02:06,100 --> 00:02:08,560
Inspectors counted about thirteen and a

66
00:02:08,560 --> 00:02:11,021
half million ounces held by the Federal

67
00:02:11,021 --> 00:02:11,893
Reserve Banks,

68
00:02:11,893 --> 00:02:13,683
almost all of it in the New

69
00:02:13,683 --> 00:02:14,730
York vault.

70
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:17,441
At the market price of September twenty

71
00:02:17,441 --> 00:02:18,348
twenty-five,

72
00:02:18,348 --> 00:02:20,676
that slice alone was worth about fifty-one

73
00:02:20,676 --> 00:02:21,817
billion dollars,

74
00:02:21,817 --> 00:02:24,145
more than four times the official value

75
00:02:24,145 --> 00:02:26,374
of all the gold combined.

76
00:02:26,617 --> 00:02:28,660
Here is a detail that surprises a

77
00:02:28,660 --> 00:02:30,077
lot of people.

78
00:02:30,297 --> 00:02:31,940
The Federal Reserve,

79
00:02:31,940 --> 00:02:33,822
America's central bank,

80
00:02:33,822 --> 00:02:36,327
does not own any of this gold.

81
00:02:36,527 --> 00:02:37,816
In its own words,

82
00:02:37,816 --> 00:02:41,047
the Federal Reserve does not own gold.

83
00:02:41,207 --> 00:02:43,347
The owner is the Treasury.

84
00:02:43,517 --> 00:02:45,434
The Fed only holds a small part

85
00:02:45,434 --> 00:02:47,711
of it for safekeeping.

86
00:02:47,950 --> 00:02:50,670
Let me draw where the gold sits.

87
00:02:50,670 --> 00:02:52,352
Here is the whole stack,

88
00:02:52,352 --> 00:02:55,390
two hundred and sixty-one million ounces.

89
00:02:55,450 --> 00:02:57,740
Fort Knox takes the biggest bar.

90
00:02:57,740 --> 00:02:59,620
Then West Point.

91
00:02:59,620 --> 00:03:01,330
Then Denver.

92
00:03:01,330 --> 00:03:03,610
And a thin slice in New York.

93
00:03:03,670 --> 00:03:06,214
More than half of America's gold sits

94
00:03:06,214 --> 00:03:09,357
behind one set of doors in Kentucky.

95
00:03:09,617 --> 00:03:11,675
So the gold is real,

96
00:03:11,675 --> 00:03:14,367
and it is enormous.

97
00:03:14,427 --> 00:03:16,413
The strange part is the price written

98
00:03:16,413 --> 00:03:17,757
next to it.

99
00:03:28,417 --> 00:03:29,637
For a long time,

100
00:03:29,637 --> 00:03:32,447
the dollar was defined in gold.

101
00:03:32,447 --> 00:03:33,956
From nineteen hundred,

102
00:03:33,956 --> 00:03:35,924
an ounce was worth twenty dollars and

103
00:03:35,924 --> 00:03:37,617
sixty-seven cents.

104
00:03:37,747 --> 00:03:39,258
In nineteen thirty-four,

105
00:03:39,258 --> 00:03:41,362
the government raised it to thirty-five

106
00:03:41,362 --> 00:03:42,717
dollars an ounce.

107
00:03:42,717 --> 00:03:44,449
That one change cut the gold value

108
00:03:44,449 --> 00:03:47,418
of the dollar to about fifty-nine percent

109
00:03:47,418 --> 00:03:49,255
of what it had been.

110
00:03:49,517 --> 00:03:52,897
Then came two laws in quick succession.

111
00:03:52,927 --> 00:03:55,539
In March nineteen seventy-two,

112
00:03:55,539 --> 00:03:57,649
Congress set the price at thirty-eight

113
00:03:57,649 --> 00:03:58,777
dollars.

114
00:03:58,887 --> 00:04:02,086
And in September nineteen seventy-three,

115
00:04:02,086 --> 00:04:04,045
it raised it one last time,

116
00:04:04,045 --> 00:04:06,877
to forty-two and two-ninths dollars.

117
00:04:07,027 --> 00:04:09,423
That is forty-two dollars and twenty-two

118
00:04:09,423 --> 00:04:10,677
cents an ounce.

119
00:04:10,877 --> 00:04:13,006
The same law also started to end

120
00:04:13,006 --> 00:04:15,971
the ban on Americans owning gold.

121
00:04:16,217 --> 00:04:17,075
And then,

122
00:04:17,075 --> 00:04:18,237
nothing.

123
00:04:18,237 --> 00:04:20,155
The market price of gold went its

124
00:04:20,155 --> 00:04:20,850
own way,

125
00:04:20,850 --> 00:04:24,327
but the official price never moved again.

126
00:04:24,347 --> 00:04:26,564
It is still written into federal law

127
00:04:26,564 --> 00:04:27,567
today.

128
00:04:27,567 --> 00:04:30,127
Every ounce in Fort Knox is booked

129
00:04:30,127 --> 00:04:33,542
at forty-two dollars and twenty-two cents.

130
00:04:33,783 --> 00:04:36,223
Let me draw two prices on the

131
00:04:36,223 --> 00:04:37,423
same chart.

132
00:04:37,423 --> 00:04:39,932
The official price climbs in small steps,

133
00:04:39,932 --> 00:04:41,959
then goes flat after nineteen

134
00:04:41,959 --> 00:04:43,573
seventy-three.

135
00:04:43,873 --> 00:04:46,383
The market price leaves it behind.

136
00:04:46,623 --> 00:04:48,519
At the end of September twenty

137
00:04:48,519 --> 00:04:49,340
twenty-five,

138
00:04:49,340 --> 00:04:51,447
the market paid about three thousand eight

139
00:04:51,447 --> 00:04:54,153
hundred and twenty-five dollars an ounce.

140
00:04:54,213 --> 00:04:55,626
By our calculation,

141
00:04:55,626 --> 00:04:58,290
that is about ninety times the official

142
00:04:58,290 --> 00:04:59,094
price.

143
00:04:59,350 --> 00:05:01,518
Why would anyone keep such an old

144
00:05:01,518 --> 00:05:02,660
number?

145
00:05:02,890 --> 00:05:04,510
Because that number is not just a

146
00:05:04,510 --> 00:05:05,470
label.

147
00:05:05,490 --> 00:05:07,532
It is tied to real money at

148
00:05:07,532 --> 00:05:09,153
the central bank.

149
00:05:14,817 --> 00:05:16,714
In nineteen thirty-four,

150
00:05:16,714 --> 00:05:19,115
the Gold Reserve Act made the Federal

151
00:05:19,115 --> 00:05:21,037
Reserve hand all its gold to the

152
00:05:21,037 --> 00:05:22,357
Treasury.

153
00:05:22,667 --> 00:05:23,531
In return,

154
00:05:23,531 --> 00:05:26,567
the Fed received gold certificates.

155
00:05:26,717 --> 00:05:28,709
They are written in dollars,

156
00:05:28,709 --> 00:05:30,978
and they cannot be swapped back for

157
00:05:30,978 --> 00:05:31,830
gold.

158
00:05:32,083 --> 00:05:34,596
The Congressional Research Service

159
00:05:34,596 --> 00:05:36,313
explains the trick.

160
00:05:36,533 --> 00:05:39,244
The Treasury monetized the gold by issuing

161
00:05:39,244 --> 00:05:41,308
those certificates to the Fed,

162
00:05:41,308 --> 00:05:43,185
and the Fed put the money into

163
00:05:43,185 --> 00:05:45,453
the Treasury's own bank account.

164
00:05:45,863 --> 00:05:47,293
But there is a limit.

165
00:05:47,293 --> 00:05:49,758
The certificates can never be worth more

166
00:05:49,758 --> 00:05:51,796
than the gold's official value.

167
00:05:52,050 --> 00:05:53,985
That is why the numbers match so

168
00:05:53,985 --> 00:05:55,310
closely.

169
00:05:55,450 --> 00:05:58,144
The Federal Reserve's weekly balance sheet

170
00:05:58,144 --> 00:06:00,390
shows a gold stock of about eleven

171
00:06:00,390 --> 00:06:01,490
billion dollars,

172
00:06:01,490 --> 00:06:04,409
and a gold certificate account of eleven

173
00:06:04,409 --> 00:06:07,030
point oh three seven billion dollars.

174
00:06:07,220 --> 00:06:09,224
The gold has already been turned into

175
00:06:09,224 --> 00:06:09,752
money,

176
00:06:09,752 --> 00:06:12,174
at the nineteen seventy-three price.

177
00:06:12,417 --> 00:06:14,277
Let me draw the loop.

178
00:06:14,277 --> 00:06:15,985
Here is the Treasury,

179
00:06:15,985 --> 00:06:17,327
with the gold.

180
00:06:17,337 --> 00:06:19,547
Here is the Federal Reserve.

181
00:06:19,827 --> 00:06:22,015
The Treasury sends certificates to the

182
00:06:22,015 --> 00:06:22,797
Fed.

183
00:06:22,917 --> 00:06:24,884
The Fed sends dollars back into the

184
00:06:24,884 --> 00:06:26,577
Treasury's account.

185
00:06:26,847 --> 00:06:29,797
The gold itself never moves.

186
00:06:29,897 --> 00:06:32,781
Only the paper and the money do.

187
00:06:33,017 --> 00:06:33,829
Of course,

188
00:06:33,829 --> 00:06:36,068
all of this only works if the

189
00:06:36,068 --> 00:06:38,067
gold is really there.

190
00:06:38,117 --> 00:06:39,785
So who checks?

191
00:06:45,450 --> 00:06:47,303
In nineteen seventy-four,

192
00:06:47,303 --> 00:06:48,948
after questions in Congress,

193
00:06:48,948 --> 00:06:51,446
the Government Accountability Office

194
00:06:51,446 --> 00:06:53,920
helped plan an audit of Fort Knox.

195
00:06:53,980 --> 00:06:56,208
Auditors checked three of the thirteen

196
00:06:56,208 --> 00:06:57,300
vault compartments,

197
00:06:57,300 --> 00:06:59,460
and found no differences.

198
00:06:59,560 --> 00:07:00,343
The next year,

199
00:07:00,343 --> 00:07:02,951
the Treasury set up a permanent committee

200
00:07:02,951 --> 00:07:04,956
to keep auditing the gold.

201
00:07:05,217 --> 00:07:06,804
By nineteen eighty-six,

202
00:07:06,804 --> 00:07:09,214
about ninety-two percent of the gold had

203
00:07:09,214 --> 00:07:10,617
been audited.

204
00:07:10,627 --> 00:07:13,057
And since nineteen ninety-five,

205
00:07:13,057 --> 00:07:15,758
the Treasury's inspector general has

206
00:07:15,758 --> 00:07:18,704
audited the gold in deep storage every

207
00:07:18,704 --> 00:07:19,661
single year,

208
00:07:19,661 --> 00:07:21,871
checking the seals on all forty-two

209
00:07:21,871 --> 00:07:23,207
compartments.

210
00:07:23,237 --> 00:07:25,685
Its latest report gave the Mint a

211
00:07:25,685 --> 00:07:27,373
clean opinion.

212
00:07:27,617 --> 00:07:30,307
Official visits make the news.

213
00:07:30,527 --> 00:07:32,714
In August twenty seventeen,

214
00:07:32,714 --> 00:07:35,909
Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin flew to

215
00:07:35,909 --> 00:07:37,875
Fort Knox with Senate leader Mitch

216
00:07:37,875 --> 00:07:38,967
McConnell.

217
00:07:39,307 --> 00:07:40,618
The trip fell on the day of

218
00:07:40,618 --> 00:07:41,723
a solar eclipse,

219
00:07:41,723 --> 00:07:44,757
and the inspector general looked into it.

220
00:07:44,907 --> 00:07:46,931
It found no indication that the date

221
00:07:46,931 --> 00:07:48,947
was chosen for the eclipse.

222
00:07:49,177 --> 00:07:51,599
The plane cost about twenty-six thousand

223
00:07:51,599 --> 00:07:53,410
nine hundred dollars.

224
00:07:53,650 --> 00:07:56,550
Some lawmakers still want more.

225
00:07:56,550 --> 00:07:58,335
In twenty twenty-five,

226
00:07:58,335 --> 00:08:01,441
a House bill proposed a full independent

227
00:08:01,441 --> 00:08:03,226
audit of all the gold,

228
00:08:03,226 --> 00:08:05,120
every five years.

229
00:08:05,180 --> 00:08:06,990
It is a proposal.

230
00:08:07,090 --> 00:08:09,357
As far as the official record we

231
00:08:09,357 --> 00:08:10,015
could check,

232
00:08:10,015 --> 00:08:11,976
it has not become law.

233
00:08:12,217 --> 00:08:14,127
Let me draw the vault.

234
00:08:14,127 --> 00:08:15,798
Forty-two compartments,

235
00:08:15,798 --> 00:08:17,367
each one sealed.

236
00:08:17,367 --> 00:08:18,428
Every year,

237
00:08:18,428 --> 00:08:20,267
inspectors check the seals.

238
00:08:20,267 --> 00:08:21,475
Once in a while,

239
00:08:21,475 --> 00:08:23,728
a compartment is opened and the bars

240
00:08:23,728 --> 00:08:25,557
are counted and tested.

241
00:08:25,597 --> 00:08:26,137
Trust,

242
00:08:26,137 --> 00:08:27,528
but verify,

243
00:08:27,528 --> 00:08:29,548
one door at a time.

244
00:08:29,783 --> 00:08:32,070
Which brings us to the most tempting

245
00:08:32,070 --> 00:08:33,813
idea of all.

246
00:08:33,983 --> 00:08:36,571
What if the government simply updated the

247
00:08:36,571 --> 00:08:37,387
price?

248
00:08:43,050 --> 00:08:44,220
On paper,

249
00:08:44,220 --> 00:08:46,020
the gap is huge.

250
00:08:46,130 --> 00:08:48,715
Booked at the market price of September

251
00:08:48,715 --> 00:08:50,101
twenty twenty-five,

252
00:08:50,101 --> 00:08:52,450
the gold would jump from about eleven

253
00:08:52,450 --> 00:08:55,400
billion dollars to about one trillion.

254
00:08:55,830 --> 00:08:58,663
The Congressional Research Service notes

255
00:08:58,663 --> 00:09:00,981
that changing the official value would

256
00:09:00,981 --> 00:09:03,814
monetize or demonetize gold through those

257
00:09:03,814 --> 00:09:06,474
certificates at the Fed.

258
00:09:06,717 --> 00:09:09,117
Some proposals go further.

259
00:09:09,117 --> 00:09:11,634
A Senate bill introduced in twenty

260
00:09:11,634 --> 00:09:12,527
twenty-five,

261
00:09:12,527 --> 00:09:13,648
the BITCOIN Act,

262
00:09:13,648 --> 00:09:15,708
would have the Treasury issue new gold

263
00:09:15,708 --> 00:09:18,202
certificates at fair market value,

264
00:09:18,202 --> 00:09:20,719
partly to help fund a national bitcoin

265
00:09:20,719 --> 00:09:21,667
reserve.

266
00:09:21,667 --> 00:09:22,503
Again,

267
00:09:22,503 --> 00:09:23,916
a proposal,

268
00:09:23,916 --> 00:09:25,093
not law.

269
00:09:25,350 --> 00:09:27,530
Here is the catch.

270
00:09:27,530 --> 00:09:30,500
Revaluing the gold does not create a

271
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:32,420
single new ounce.

272
00:09:32,920 --> 00:09:35,258
It changes a number on a page,

273
00:09:35,258 --> 00:09:37,619
and lets the government turn that number

274
00:09:37,619 --> 00:09:40,580
into fresh money at the central bank.

275
00:09:40,890 --> 00:09:43,410
Supporters see a hidden asset.

276
00:09:43,640 --> 00:09:45,958
Critics see printing money with extra

277
00:09:45,958 --> 00:09:46,790
steps.

278
00:09:47,100 --> 00:09:48,147
Either way,

279
00:09:48,147 --> 00:09:51,100
the gold in Kentucky stays exactly where

280
00:09:51,100 --> 00:09:52,237
it is.

281
00:09:52,483 --> 00:09:55,293
And it is a lot of gold.

282
00:09:55,433 --> 00:09:58,052
According to World Gold Council figures,

283
00:09:58,052 --> 00:09:59,571
based on IMF data,

284
00:09:59,571 --> 00:10:01,552
China holds around two thousand three

285
00:10:01,552 --> 00:10:03,731
hundred and eighty-seven tonnes,

286
00:10:03,731 --> 00:10:06,153
and Russia about two thousand two hundred

287
00:10:06,153 --> 00:10:07,633
and seventy.

288
00:10:07,753 --> 00:10:09,222
By our calculation,

289
00:10:09,222 --> 00:10:11,565
the United States holds about three point

290
00:10:11,565 --> 00:10:13,868
four times as much as China.

291
00:10:14,117 --> 00:10:16,917
And other countries are still buying.

292
00:10:17,057 --> 00:10:19,412
